-- RayValue: Germany (DE) and Australia (AU) solar + battery calculator data. Second and third non-US calculators, on the
-- shared international island (/de/photovoltaik-rechner, /au/solar-battery-calculator), both hidden until the owner signs off.
-- APPLY ORDER: after 0036 (needs 0035: vat_rates, calculator_locations, the 'flat' rate_type). Additive only.
-- NOT applied by the commit that adds this file: the owner applies it.
--
-- Every number below has a source_url + verified_on, or is a labelled product-choice assumption with a rationale. Where a source is
-- SECONDARY (CHOICE, SolarQuotes) the row's note says so in capitals. Retrieved / verified on 2026-11-22.
--
-- DE: household price (Eurostat nrg_pc_204 2026-S1), EEG feed-in rates 1 Aug - 31 Dec 2026 (Bundesnetzagentur, date-effective), 0% VAT
--     (section 12(3) UStG), costs (Verbraucherzentrale NRW offer evaluation, Stand 3 Nov 2026), PVGIS yield points = the 16 Land capitals.
-- AU: AER Default Market Offer 2026-27 flat tariffs for 5 networks + Synergy A1 (WA) + Jacana (NT), per-state feed-in offers chosen by
--     the user, the Cheaper Home Batteries rebate as a date-effective incentive (STC factor 6.8 to 31 Dec 2026, 5.7 from 1 Jan 2027),
--     costs from SolarQuotes / CHOICE (SECONDARY, flagged), PVGIS yield points per network.
-- No incentive row for DE (no universal grant; the KfW loan is a loan). The AU small-scale solar STC discount is NOT a separate incentive
-- row: SolarQuotes prices are what buyers pay (after the point-of-sale STC discount), so deducting it again would double count.

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ schema: no change (0035 added vat_rates / calculator_locations)

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ markets + regions

update public.markets set
  prices_include_tax = true,
  display_round = 50,
  display_round_rationale = 'Assumption: round money to the nearest EUR 50 so that small homes (savings of a few hundred euros a year) are not rounded to the nearest EUR 100.',
  vat_note = 'VAT on installed PV and storage is 0% for private homes up to 30 kWp (section 12(3) UStG), no end date in the statute: see public.vat_rates. markets.vat_rate stays null on purpose (the calculator uses the date-effective table).'
where code = 'DE';

update public.markets set
  prices_include_tax = true,
  display_round = 50,
  display_round_rationale = 'Assumption: round money to the nearest AUD 50 so that small homes (savings of a few hundred dollars a year) are not rounded to the nearest AUD 100.',
  vat_note = 'Australian prices are quoted including GST; cost rows are tax-inclusive (includes_tax = true), so no GST schedule is needed and markets.vat_rate stays null.'
where code = 'AU';

on conflict (country, code) do nothing;

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ DE utilities: one row per Bundesland, all with the national Eurostat price
-- Eurostat nrg_pc_204, Germany, 2026-S1, consumption band DC (2,500-4,999 kWh/yr), all taxes and levies included: EUR 0.3618 per kWh.
-- The price is national, so every Bundesland row carries the same figure; the Bundesland only selects the PVGIS yield point (see calculator_locations).
-- fixed_monthly_charge = 0: Eurostat publishes one all-in price per kWh for the band and we hold no sourced Grundpreis, so no separate fixed charge is modelled
-- (an open item for the owner: a missing fact, not a null column, so the generated docs/TODO-VERIFY.md does not list it). BDEW (secondary, industry body) gives about 37 ct/kWh for new customers; not used.

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ DE export offers (EEG feed-in, Bundesnetzagentur)
-- Rates for systems commissioned 1 Aug - 31 Dec 2026, up to 10 kW (the calculator caps the system at 10 kW). Fixed by law, so low = likely = high (no spread).
-- After 31 Dec 2026 there is no rate row on purpose: the Bundesnetzagentur says a successor rule is "in der parlamentarischen Beratung" (EEG-Novelle 2027, Drs. 21/7867, not passed
-- on 2026-11-22), so the calculator offers installs up to 31 Dec 2026 only (model_assumptions install_horizon) and does not guess a 2027 tariff.
-- Not modelled (stated in the notes): the Solarspitzengesetz 60% feed-in-power cap for systems without a smart meter (section 9(2) EEG) and the loss of payment in negative-price hours.


-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ DE VAT (0% on PV and storage for private homes up to 30 kWp)
-- section 12(3) UStG: "Die Steuer ermäßigt sich auf 0 Prozent" for supply and installation of solar modules and storage for private dwellings; deemed met up to 30 kW(peak) per the
-- Marktstammdatenregister. The statute text shows no end date. The calculator caps the system at 10 kW, so the 30 kWp threshold is never exceeded.

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ DE installed costs (Verbraucherzentrale NRW, Stand 3 Nov 2026)
-- Evaluation of 71 real PV offers and 70 real battery offers (Energieberater:innen der Verbraucherzentrale NRW), January 2025 - August 2026. The page publishes
-- AVERAGES per size band (not medians); PV prices are net (no VAT since 1 Jan 2023). Excludes battery, backup power and extra electrical work.
-- PV bands up to 10 kW (the calculator caps the system at 10 kW): 4-6 kWp 2,090; >6-8 kWp 1,820; >8-9 kWp 1,520; >9-10 kWp 1,410 EUR per kWp.
-- Storage (installed together with PV): 4-6 kWh 510; >6-8 kWh 480; >8-10 kWh 410; >10-12 kWh 390; >12-16 kWh 360 EUR per kWh.

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ DE assumptions (product choices, not facts: each has a rationale)

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ DE calculator locations (PVGIS yield point per Bundesland = its capital)
-- Product choice: the Land capital stands for the Bundesland. Coordinates are Wikidata's (the same ones the place pages use; Hamburg and Berlin from Wikidata Q1055 / Q64).

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ AU utilities
-- 5 networks: AER Default Market Offer 2026-27 residential FLAT-rate tariff caps (final determination, Figure 2.1), incl. GST, valid 1 Jul 2026 - 30 Jun 2027. They are the
-- MAXIMUM a retailer can charge on a standing offer, not the price of a market offer; most households pay somewhat different rates. The AER site blocks bots, so the PDF was read from a
-- web.archive.org snapshot (research pack). Also Synergy A1 (WA, Energy Policy WA) and Jacana Energy Standard Residential (NT). Victoria is NOT included: the ESC publishes
-- only annual bills at 4,000 kWh (no unit rate / supply charge split), so there is no flat rate to use (open item for the owner).
-- fixed_monthly_charge = c/day x 365 / 12 / 100, to the cent.

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ AU export offers (user-chosen: the household picks the feed-in rate it is, or expects to be, paid)
-- Australian feed-in rates are set per state / retailer. The calculator lists these sourced benchmark, regulated and minimum rates and the user chooses; retailer plans can pay more (see Energy Made Easy).
-- All are 'feed_in' (exports paid a flat per-kWh rate; self-consumed solar saves the usage rate). Time-of-day rates (IPART 17-33 c in the evening, WA DEBS 10 c 3-9 pm) are NOT modelled: the engine holds one flat rate.
-- Statutory / regulated rates: low = likely = high. IPART publishes a range: low/high are its ends and likely is the arithmetic midpoint (derived, stated in the note).


-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ AU incentive: Cheaper Home Batteries Program (federal), date-effective
-- The rebate is paid as Small-scale Technology Certificates (STCs): usable kWh x the STC factor for the date the battery is certified for compliance, tiered 100% of the factor for the
-- first 14 kWh, 60% for >14-28 kWh, 15% for >28-50 kWh, total rounded down (Renewable Energy (Electricity) Regulations reg 20(8), amended by F2026L00093). Factor 6.8 for 1 May - 31 Dec 2026, 5.7 for 1 Jan - 30 Jun 2027.
-- Dollar value = STCs x the STC price. STC price, PRIMARY: the Clean Energy Regulator Quarterly Carbon Market Report (March quarter 2026) states the STC spot price opened 2026 at AUD 39.65, was AUD 39.62 on
-- 15 May 2026, and the fixed Clearing House price is AUD 40. likely = spot 39.62 x factor; high = Clearing House 40 x factor; low = likely less the 10% admin fee CHOICE (SECONDARY) says to expect.
-- The modelled battery is 10 kWh (model_assumptions battery_usable_kwh), inside the first tier, so the tiers do not change the amount. Installs after 30 Jun 2027 are not offered (install_horizon).
-- STC price risk: the spot price moves; amounts use the spot price of mid-May 2026 for 2027 as well. The calculator uses the install date as the certification date.

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ AU installed costs (SECONDARY sources, flagged)
-- No primary source for Australian installed prices was found (Solar Choice Price Index returns 403 to bots; the CER publishes no prices). Both rows come from consumer/lead-generation sites and are flagged as secondary
-- in the note and on the results page.
-- Solar: SolarQuotes "How much do solar panels cost" (last updated 12 Oct 2026), typical fully installed prices buyers reported paying: 5 kW AUD 5,000-7,500; 6.6 kW 6,000-9,000; 8 kW 7,500-11,000; 10 kW 9,000-12,000, and a
--   rule of thumb of ~AUD 1,000 per kW. low = cheapest per-watt end of the 5-10 kW rows (10 kW low end, 9,000 / 10,000 = 0.90); likely = the rule of thumb 1.00; high = dearest per-watt end (5 kW high end, 7,500 / 5,000 = 1.50).
--   The page does not say whether the STC discount is already deducted; buyers pay the post-STC price, so it is treated as post-STC and tax-inclusive, and no separate solar STC incentive is applied.
-- Battery: CHOICE (prices from SolarQuotes partners): 10 kWh storage AUD 7,000-11,000 installed AFTER the federal rebate (at the 2026 STC value CHOICE prints: AUD 272 per kWh = 6.8 STC x AUD 40). The engine deducts the rebate
--   itself, so the cost row is the price BEFORE the rebate = CHOICE after-rebate price + 10 kWh x 272 = + AUD 2,720: low 9,720; high 13,720; likely = the midpoint 11,720 (derived). With the 2026 rebate the net price therefore
--   equals CHOICE's after-rebate range; from 1 Jan 2027 the lower factor raises it.

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ AU assumptions (product choices, not facts: each has a rationale)

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ AU calculator locations (PVGIS yield point per network)
-- Product choice: one representative place per network (the capital, or a regional centre for Essential Energy and a western Sydney centre for Endeavour). The place stands for the whole network: yield differs within it.
-- PVGIS gets the equator-facing (north-facing) aspect in the southern hemisphere (src/lib/server/pvgis.ts). Coordinates are Wikipedia's, as in the place pages.

notify pgrst, 'reload schema';
